<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William Dunbar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1460–c. 1520</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William Dunbar was a Scottish poet at the court of James IV, the most accomplished of the makars writing in Scots. His varied output includes The Thrissil and the Rois, celebrating the king's marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-tudor">Margaret Tudor</a> in 1503, comic and satirical verse and the Lament for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/makari">Makaris</a>, a meditation on death that names earlier poets. He disappears from the records after 1513.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-dunbar.jpg" alt="William Dunbar" width="320" height="481" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Stephencdickson · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_of_William_Dunbar,_Scottish_National_Portrait_Gallery.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
